Overview

Two independent pipelines classify speech samples as Control or Dementia:

Text Model	Audio Model
Input	CHAT-format (.cha) transcript	Raw audio recording
Features	11 hand-crafted linguistic features	88 eGeMAPS acoustic features (openSMILE)
Algorithm	Logistic Regression	SVM (RBF kernel)
Accuracy	76.3% ± 3.8% (5-fold stratified CV)	61.8% ± 2.5% (5-fold speaker-grouped CV)
Deployed pipeline	transcript_files/model/logistic_regression_pipeline.pkl	model/audio_model.pkl

The two models are not fused into a single prediction — see Key Design Decisions.

Models & Results
📝 Text Model

11 linguistic features per transcript, extracted via transcript_files/extraction_files.py:

info_units_mentioned, avg_word_length, avg_dependency_tree_depth,
avg_sentence_length, pronoun_ratio, n_repetitions, n_vague_words,
n_pauses, mtld, clause_density, semantic_coherence

Trained as StandardScaler + LogisticRegression in an sklearn Pipeline. Validated with 5-fold stratified cross-validation.

🎙️ Audio Model

88 eGeMAPS functional features (pitch, loudness, spectral, and timing statistics), extracted with openSMILE via scripts/extract_features.py.

Trained as StandardScaler + SVC(kernel='rbf'). Validated with 5-fold speaker-grouped cross-validation (no speaker appears in both train and test folds — prevents inflated accuracy).

Exploratory comparisons across Logistic Regression / Random Forest / SVM on the acoustic features (see modelevaluation/, which generates model_comparison.png, roc_curves.png, confusion_matrices.png, rf_feature_importance.png) informed the choice of SVM for deployment.

